example.paragraph:
Let $U = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{cr}
       & \\
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{2}} & {2\text{/}3} \\
      {1\text{/}\sqrt{2}} & {- 2\text{/}3} \\
      0 & {1\text{/}3}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\textbf{x} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      \sqrt{2} \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Notice that $U$ has orthonormal columns and

statement.paragraph:
Compute the orthogonal projection of $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      1 \\
      {- 1}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ onto the line through $\left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      {- 1} \\
      2
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and the origin.

Let $\textbf{y} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      2 \\
      3
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$ and $\mathbf{u} = \left\lbrack \begin{array}{r}
      4 \\
      {- 7}
      \end{array} \right\rbrack$. Write $\textbf{y}$ as the sum of two orthogonal vectors, one in $\text{Span~}\left\{ \mathbf{u} \right\}$ and one orthogonal to $\mathbf{u}$.
